The following instructions apply to building the macOS Metal build of RetroArch,
which is the recommended build for testing on a modern Mac (macOS 10.13 or later).

Prerequisites:
    - macOS 10.13 (High Sierra) or later
    - Xcode (latest version recommended; install from the Mac App Store)
    - Xcode Command Line Tools: run 'xcode-select --install'
    - Git

All commands below should be run from the root of the RetroArch source tree.

Quick start — Xcode IDE:
    1. Open the workspace in Xcode:
           open pkg/apple/RetroArch.xcworkspace
    2. In the scheme selector at the top of the Xcode window, choose the
       "RetroArch" scheme and target "My Mac".
    3. Choose Product -> Build (or press Cmd+B) to build, or
       Product -> Run (Cmd+R) to build and launch immediately.
    4. The built app can be found in Xcode's derived data directory:
           ~/Library/Developer/Xcode/DerivedData/RetroArch*/Build/Products/Debug/RetroArch.app

Quick start — command line (no code signing required):
    Run the following command from the repository root:

        xcodebuild \
            -workspace pkg/apple/RetroArch.xcworkspace \
            -scheme RetroArch \
            -config Release \
            -xcconfig pkg/apple/GitHubCI.xcconfig \
            -derivedDataPath build

    The resulting app will be at:
        build/Build/Products/Release/RetroArch.app

    To run it immediately after building:
        open build/Build/Products/Release/RetroArch.app

Build configurations:
    The Xcode project provides several build targets:

    RetroArch         — Standard Metal build for local development and testing.
                        Uses RetroArch.entitlements (allows JIT, dylib loading, etc.)
                        and Metal.xcconfig for graphics settings.

    RetroArch AppStore — App Store distribution build.
                        Uses AppStore.xcconfig which enables iCloud, iCloud Drive
                        and App Store-specific features. Requires valid Apple
                        Developer credentials and provisioning profiles.

    RetroArchSteam    — Steam-specific build (requires the Mist Steam library).

    RetroArchQt       — Qt-based UI variant (requires Qt framework).

    For local testing, always use the "RetroArch" target.

Code signing:
    The GitHubCI.xcconfig configuration (used in the command-line example above)
    disables code signing so no Apple Developer account is required.

    If building via the Xcode IDE, Xcode will automatically sign the app with
    your local development certificate. If you see code signing errors, go to
    the project settings (Signing & Capabilities tab) and set your team.

    The app uses RetroArch.entitlements, which grants the following permissions
    needed for emulation:
        - JIT compilation (allow-jit, allow-unsigned-executable-memory,
          disable-executable-page-protection)
        - Loading unsigned cores (disable-library-validation,
          allow-dyld-environment-variables)
        - Audio/camera input

    Note: On Apple Silicon Macs, JIT requires the process to be debugged or
    for the app to have the com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit entitlement, which
    RetroArch.entitlements already provides.

Libretro cores:
    RetroArch needs at least one libretro core to run content. Prebuilt cores
    can be downloaded inside the app via Main Menu -> Online Updater -> Core
    Downloader, or obtained from https://buildbot.libretro.com/nightly/apple/osx/

    To build a core from source, refer to the individual core's documentation.
    Cores are .dylib files and should be placed in:
        ~/Library/Application Support/RetroArch/cores/
    or configured via Settings -> Directory -> Cores in the RetroArch UI.

Troubleshooting:
    - "No signing certificate found": Set your development team in Xcode's
      project settings, or use GitHubCI.xcconfig to skip signing entirely.

    - Build fails with "xcpretty: command not found": The xcpretty formatter
      is optional. Remove '| xcpretty --color' from the xcodebuild command.

    - App crashes on launch on older macOS: The minimum deployment target is
      macOS 10.13. Ensure your system meets this requirement.

    - Cores fail to load ("The code signature is invalid"): Make sure the app
      is signed with the entitlements file that includes
      com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation.
